(the same file is mirrored to pulp/pulp/chips/flex_cluster/ and install/generators/pulp/chips/flex_cluster/)
Base: working tree AFTER the earlier float_to_fp16 round-to-nearest-even fix (kept unchanged).
Build: cmake --build build --target gen_pulp_chips_flex_cluster_light_redmule_cpp_219482712_{optim,debug}
       (no CMake change: the fp16 code uses __attribute__((target("arch=armv8.2-a+fp16"))) on the
       functions that need it, so the shared flags stay "-g -O3 -fPIC -fno-stack-protector").
Installed .so: install/models/gen_pulp_..._219482712.so (+ debug/); previous builds kept as
       .so.orig (pre-RNE fix) and .so.pre-neon (RNE fix only).

What changed (only the arithmetic helpers at the bottom of the file + an fp8 LUT; no address
generation / timing code touched):
  * matmul_fp16: AArch64 NEON path (vfmaq_f16, 8 output columns per vector, 4x32 register block,
    accumulator kept in fp16 lanes -> one fp16 rounding per MAC exactly like before), column tail
    via zero-padded lanes, runtime HWCAP check (FPHP+ASIMDHP) with the original scalar code as
    fallback; REDMULE_NO_NEON=1 in the environment forces the scalar path for A/B checks.
  * matmul_{u,}int{8,16}: same loops, but accumulate a 64-column block in a local uint32 array so
    GCC auto-vectorises; wrap-around per step == one wrap at the end, results bit-identical.
  * fp8e4m3_fma: fp8->float through a 256-entry table built from the original converter.

Semantics vs. the scalar model (fp16):
  * Old: a*b exact in fp32, (+c) rounded to fp32, then rounded RNE to fp16 (double rounding).
    New: fused a*b+c with ONE RNE rounding to fp16 (what FPnew's fp16 FMA does, so closer to RTL).
    Measured against an exact (long double) FMA + single RNE rounding: the NEON result matched it in
    100% of ~30k checked outputs, the old path differed in 0.01% (gemm --real data) to 0.13%
    (random normal fp16 bits), by up to a few ulp after propagation through the accumulation.
    On the integer-valued data of tests/gvsoc/run.py gemm the two are bit-identical.
  * Subnormal / inf / NaN now follow IEEE fp16 (FPCR.FZ16 = 0, no flush).  The old fp16_to_float
    read fp16 subnormals as ~0 (so a subnormal *accumulator* was zeroed on the next MAC) and read
    inf/NaN as the finite value 2^16*(1.m); the new path keeps subnormals (|x| < 6.1e-5) and
    propagates inf/NaN.  Only matters for data that overflows/underflows fp16.

Host rounding mode (found by the siglip test, first NEON build failed H/G/OUT with errors up to 1.2):
  the Snitch ISS (core/models/cpu/iss/include/isa_lib/int.h) emulates RISC-V rounding modes with
  fesetround() and can leave the engine thread in RTZ/RDN/RUP.  The scalar path never noticed
  (its final fp16 rounding is hand-written RNE; only the fp32 add was affected), vfmaq_f16 honours
  FPCR, so P.V (softmax probabilities, 256-term accumulation) came out ~4 % low under RTZ.  The
  kernel now saves FPCR, forces RMode=RN, FZ=FZ16=0, and restores it afterwards (verified against
  the exact reference with the host set to FE_TOWARDZERO).

Kernel measurement (standalone, host tile 128 x 2048 x 128 = 33.5 MMAC, same -O3 flags):
    scalar model : 14.35 ns/MAC   (0.48 s per process_compute call; ~25 ns/MAC inside gvsoc)
    NEON fp16    : 0.076 ns/MAC   (2.6 ms per call)  -> ~190x
